  "strategy": "Approach and rationale: Our approach is to directly minimize the overlap integral using a smart population-based optimization algorithm. By representing the function as a vector of active/inactive intervals and using a well-tuned genetic algorithm with repair mechanisms, we escape local minima and find globally better solutions than the parent method. The key novel aspect is (1) using a compact representation for the function, (2) fixing the repair mechanism to maintain integral=1 exactly, (3) eliminating need for random intermediate steps by focusing on optimization and repair, and (4) including direct use of the parent solution as a seed when available.\n\nOptimization will be more robust than parent's complex gradient/perturbation method, achieving lower C5 through:\n- Better handling of the integral constraint (avoid clipping approach)\n- More systematic exploration of the solution space\n- Improved crossover and mutation operations balanced for exploration/exploitation\n\nThe representation limits search to valid solutions avoiding infeasibility, and the repair mechanism guarantees strict adherence to constraints at every optimization step. This is a significant improvement over parent's multiple approximations.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise linear approximation with two humps per period (400 points \u2192 100 segments)\n- Derived representation variables: Start and end positions of humps (400-dimensional)\n- Objective function: C5 = dx * max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (dx=2/400)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u22641 for all i, \u2211h[i]*dx =1\n- Repair mechanism enforces both constraints exactly at every step\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization: \n   - If parent solution available, convert to binary vector and mutate slightly\n   - Else generate valid samples by combining two active intervals per period\n2. Selection:\n   - Select k individuals for crossover proportionally to fitness\n   - Apply tournament selection with multiplier 1.2\n3. Crossover:\n   - Single interval crossover (probability 0.9)\n   - Segment matching crossover\n4. Mutation:\n   - Swap active segments between random intervals (probability p=0.3)\n   - Apply repair to maintain integral=1\n5. Repair:\n   - Project violating constraints using linear adjustment\n   - Explorative phase alters function shape with random step\n6. Termination:\n   - Max time 1300 seconds\n   - Track best candidate through time\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- The population learns from parent example when possible boosts exploration\n- Multiple crossover variants controlled by probability\n- Mutation rate adapts gradually from 0.5 to 0.1 over iterations\n- Repair strength increases when approaching solution boundary\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Max evals: 500 calls to objective function (<150 s)\n- Repair and crossover time <0.5 * evals\n- Safeguards against:\n  - Overpopulation: dynamic sizing with avg fitness\n  - Divergence: restart condition if all candidates decay\n  - Fitness explosion: check objective value < previous best+0.05\n- Fallback: Return initial candidate if major failure\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- As re-assurance validate:\n  - h[i] between 0 and 1\n  - \u2211h[i]*dx=1 (repair ensures)\n  - C5 computed exactly and matches parent evaluator\n  - Format: Tuple(h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nThe algorithm block is self-contained and can start/terminate within time. The repair projection is exact and ensures no infeasible points ever submit.",
